This page exists to be looked at. It is one page carrying every element a skin has to style, so
that a skin author can bake it and see the whole surface at once instead of discovering, three
sites later, that nobody ever gave the definition lists a margin. Nothing here is documentation;
if a sentence reads like filler, it is filler, and it is here because running text is the only
way to judge a measure and a line height.

Bake this directory with your own skin listed in <code>.wikven.yaml</code>. It sets
<code>WikvenSkinPreview</code>, so wikven leaves the chrome alone and what you see is your skin's
own work: its personal menu, its tabs, its toolbox. See
[[Second page]] for what a second page and a sidebar entry look like, and
[[Second page/A subpage with a rather long title]] for a subpage.

== Headings ==

The heading above is a level two, which is the first one a page body can hold: the level one is
the page title, drawn by the skin rather than by the wikitext. What follows is the rest of the
ladder, which is worth seeing in one column because the gap between a level four and a level five
is where a type scale usually gives up.

=== Level three ===

Sits under a level two, and most skins still give it a rule or a weight of its own.

==== Level four ====

By here a skin has usually run out of size and started using weight or colour instead.

===== Level five =====

Rare in real pages, common in the templates that generate them.

====== Level six ======

The last one MediaWiki has. If it is indistinguishable from bold body text, that is a decision
rather than an accident, but it should be a decision.

== Running text ==

A paragraph of ordinary prose, long enough to wrap several times at any sensible measure, because
a line height that looks generous on two lines can look loose on eight. The words are chosen to be
unremarkable. Their job is to be a grey block at arm's length and a readable one up close, and a
skin that gets the first right and the second wrong is a skin that was only ever looked at in a
screenshot.

A second paragraph, to show what a skin does with the space between two of them. Some skins
separate paragraphs with a margin and some with an indent, and the two look very different once
there are five in a row. This one also runs long enough to wrap, since the interesting question is
not what one line does but what the block does.

A third, shorter one, because not every paragraph is a wall.

=== Inline formatting ===

Text can be '''bold''', ''italic'', '''''both at once''''', <s>struck through</s>, <small>small</small>,
<code>monospaced</code>, or carry a <sup>superscript</sup> and a <sub>subscript</sub>. It can hold an
[[Second page|internal link]], a [[A page that does not exist|red link to a page that does not
exist]], an [https://www.mediawiki.org/ external link], and an external link with
[https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Manual:Contents no visible protocol]. A skin that colours these
four identically has thrown away information the reader uses.

It can also hold a token no line break will fit:
<code>ThisIsAnUnreasonablyLongIdentifierNobodyWouldWriteButSomebodyAlwaysDoes</code>. That is here
because an unbreakable run is what puts a horizontal scrollbar on a phone, and the only way to know
whether your skin wraps it is to look at it.

== Lists ==

* A bulleted list.
* Its second item.
** A nested one, which is where list indentation either reads or does not.
*** And a third level, since templates generate them.
* Back to the top level.

# A numbered list.
# Its second item.
## Nested, and numbered separately.
## Which is the case that catches a skin styling only the outer counter.
# Back to the top level.

* A bulleted item with a numbered list under it:
*# because mixed nesting is legal
*# and skins disagree about it

; A definition list
: The term above, and this description. Definition lists carry the glossaries, the parameter
  tables that were not tables, and half the templates on any wiki, and they are the element most
  often left with browser defaults.
; A second term
: With its own description, so the spacing between two pairs is visible.

== Tables ==

{| class="wikitable"
|+ A table with a caption, which skins style separately from the table
! Header !! Another header !! A third
|-
| A cell || Another cell || A third
|-
| A row with a longer cell in it, to see what the table does when one column wants more room than
the others || Short || Short
|-
| Last row || Last row || Last row
|}

A table can also float, which is what every infobox on every wiki is:

{| class="wikitable" style="float: right; margin-left: 1em; max-width: 20em;"
|+ A floating table
! Field
| A value
|-
! Another field
| Another value, long enough to wrap inside a narrow float
|-
! A third
| A value
|}

And here is the paragraph that has to flow around it. It needs to be long enough to actually reach
past the bottom of the float, because the interesting failure is not the text beside the box but
the heading that comes after it and forgets to clear. Skins that set a clearfix on headings look
fine here; skins that do not will show it in the next section, which is exactly why this paragraph
goes on for as long as it does rather than stopping at a polite two lines.

== Images ==

[[File:Specimen.svg|thumb|right|A thumbnail floated right, with a caption long enough to wrap
inside the frame.]]

A thumbnail is the most-styled image element on a wiki: it has a frame, a caption, a float, and a
magnify affordance, and each of them is somebody's stylesheet. The paragraph beside it is here so
the float has something to float against, and it runs on for a few lines so that the wrap is
visible rather than theoretical.

[[File:Specimen.svg|thumb|left|And one floated left, which is the case skins forget.]]

Left floats are rarer and therefore less tested. The text beside this one exists for the same
reason as the text beside the last one, and if the two paragraphs read alike that is because their
job is the same.

<div style="clear: both;"></div>

A frameless image, inline with the text: [[File:Specimen.svg|frameless|60px]] and the sentence
carries on after it, which is where baseline alignment either works or does not.

